The code below downloads about 80 MB from GEO and runs in a few minutes.*

# Why give DecontX the empty droplets?

Without empty droplets, DecontX learns the contamination of each cell from
the cells themselves: the contamination profile of a cluster is a mixture
of the other clusters. That works when each cell type is mostly clean, so
its markers stand out against the other cell types. It works less well
when:

- the contamination is high in every cluster, so the profile built from
  the other clusters is itself contaminated, or
- much of the ambient RNA comes from cells or debris that aren't among the
  called cells, such as cells that lysed during dissociation, or the
  cytoplasm and mitochondria stripped away when nuclei are isolated.

The empty droplets hold the ambient RNA itself. With the `background`
argument, DecontX takes the contamination profile from them instead of
from the other clusters.

Single-nucleus data from frozen tissue often fit both cases. This article
runs DecontX with and without the empty droplets on one lung sample from a
COVID-19 autopsy study and compares the two. The
[DecontX vignette](decontX_RNA.html)
covers the basics, the plotting functions, and a FAQ.

The article needs these Bioconductor packages besides decontX:
DropletUtils (to read the counts and call cells), BiocFileCache (to
download and cache the data) and SingleCellExperiment, plus R.utils and
ggplot2 from CRAN.

```{r packages}
library(decontX)
library(DropletUtils)
library(BiocFileCache)
library(SingleCellExperiment)
library(ggplot2)
```

# The data

Delorey et al. (2021) profiled autopsy tissue from COVID-19 patients,
including single-nucleus RNA-seq of lung (10x Genomics 3' v3 chemistry).
GEO series [GSE171668](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE171668)
has, for each sample, the raw matrix from CellRanger, which holds every
droplet barcode, and the output of CellBender, another method for removing
ambient RNA. We use one lung sample, 02-P005175-S053-R01 (donor D1). The
files are gzipped, so we download them once into the BiocFileCache cache
and unzip a copy into a temporary folder:

```{r download}
bfc <- BiocFileCache(ask = FALSE)
geo <- "https://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/samples/GSM5229nnn/"
rawUrl <- paste0(
  geo, "GSM5229973/suppl/",
  "GSM5229973_02-P005175-S053-R01_raw_feature_bc_matrix.h5.gz"
)
rawH5 <- R.utils::gunzip(
  bfcrpath(bfc, rawUrl),
  destname = tempfile(fileext = ".h5"),
  remove = FALSE
)

raw <- read10xCounts(rawH5, col.names = TRUE, type = "HDF5")
counts(raw) <- as(counts(raw), "dgCMatrix")
raw <- raw[, colSums(counts(raw)) > 0]
dim(raw)
```

The gene symbols carry the prefix of the reference genome
(`GRCh38premrna_`), which we remove. A few symbols are duplicated, so we
make them unique:

```{r gene_names}
rownames(raw) <- make.unique(sub("^GRCh38premrna_", "", rowData(raw)$Symbol))
```

# Calling nuclei

`emptyDropsCellRanger()` separates droplets holding a nucleus from empty
droplets, the way CellRanger does. It is random, so we set a seed:

```{r call_nuclei}
set.seed(2021)
calls <- emptyDropsCellRanger(counts(raw))
isNucleus <- !is.na(calls$FDR) & calls$FDR <= 0.01
sum(isNucleus)
```

The droplets that weren't called are the empty droplets, which we give to
DecontX as the background:

```{r empty}
empty <- raw[, !isNucleus]
summary(colSums(counts(empty)))
```

Most hold only a few counts, but there are hundreds of thousands of them,
so together they give a good estimate of the ambient RNA.

The called nuclei still include damaged ones. Nuclei should hold few
mitochondrial transcripts, so a high share of them points to cytoplasm
stuck to the nucleus, or to ambient RNA. We keep the nuclei with less than
5% mitochondrial counts:

```{r qc}
nuclei <- raw[, isNucleus]
isMito <- grepl("^MT-", rownames(nuclei))
total <- colSums(counts(nuclei))
mitoPercent <- colSums(counts(nuclei)[isMito, ]) / total * 100
quantile(mitoPercent, c(0.5, 0.75, 0.9))
nuclei <- nuclei[, mitoPercent < 5]
ncol(nuclei)
median(colSums(counts(nuclei)))
```

The nuclei that fail QC are left out of both `nuclei` and `empty`: they
aren't cells we want to decontaminate, and they aren't empty either.

# DecontX with and without the empty droplets

First, DecontX on its own. It clusters the nuclei and estimates each
nucleus's contamination from the other clusters:

```{r decontx_no_background}
noBackground <- decontX(nuclei)
table(noBackground$decontX_clusters)
```

Then with the empty droplets. We pass the clusters from the first run as
`z`, so that the empty droplets are the only thing that changes between
the two runs:

```{r decontx_background}
withBackground <- decontX(
  nuclei,
  z = noBackground$decontX_clusters,
  background = empty
)
```

## Naming the clusters

To name the clusters, we score each one with the markers of the main cell
types in the lung: the mean log-normalized expression of each marker set,
scaled across clusters. Each cluster takes the cell type it scores highest
for:

```{r cell_types}
cellTypeMarkers <- list(
  Epithelial = c("SFTPC", "SFTPB", "NAPSA", "LAMP3", "AGER"),
  Ciliated = c("FOXJ1", "CAPS", "TPPP3"),
  Myeloid = c("MARCO", "CD163", "C1QA", "MRC1"),
  Lymphoid = c("CD3E", "CD2", "NKG7", "MS4A1"),
  Fibroblast = c("COL1A2", "DCN", "LUM"),
  Endothelial = c("PECAM1", "VWF", "CLDN5"),
  SmoothMuscle = c("ACTA2", "MYH11", "TAGLN")
)
z <- noBackground$decontX_clusters
x <- counts(nuclei)
logNorm <- log1p(t(t(x) / colSums(x)) * 1e4)
score <- sapply(cellTypeMarkers, function(genes) {
  tapply(colMeans(as.matrix(logNorm[genes, ])), z, mean)
})
score <- scale(score)
round(score, 1)
clusterType <- setNames(colnames(score)[apply(score, 1, which.max)],
                        rownames(score))
clusterType
nuclei$cellType <- unname(clusterType[as.character(z)])
```

Each cluster scores clearly highest for one cell type. With other data,
check the scores, and look at more markers when two cell types come close.

# What changes with the empty droplets

## The amount of contamination

```{r contamination_summary}
estimates <- data.frame(
  cellType = nuclei$cellType,
  noBackground = noBackground$decontX_contamination,
  withBackground = withBackground$decontX_contamination
)
summary(estimates[, c("noBackground", "withBackground")])
```

```{r contamination_by_type, fig.width = 8, fig.height = 4}
long <- data.frame(
  cellType = rep(estimates$cellType, 2),
  run = factor(
    rep(c("Without empty droplets", "With empty droplets"),
        each = nrow(estimates)),
    levels = c("Without empty droplets", "With empty droplets")
  ),
  contamination = c(estimates$noBackground, estimates$withBackground)
)
ggplot(long, aes(cellType, contamination, fill = run)) +
  geom_boxplot(outlier.size = 0.3) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Estimated contamination", fill = NULL) +
  theme_bw() +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 30, hjust = 1))
```

```{r contamination_scatter, fig.width = 6, fig.height = 5}
ggplot(estimates, aes(noBackground, withBackground, color = cellType)) +
  geom_point(size = 0.4, alpha = 0.5) +
  geom_abline(linetype = "dashed") +
  guides(color = guide_legend(override.aes = list(size = 3, alpha = 1))) +
  labs(
    x = "Contamination without empty droplets",
    y = "Contamination with empty droplets",
    color = NULL
  ) +
  theme_bw()
cor(estimates$noBackground, estimates$withBackground)
```

The run with the empty droplets finds more contamination in most cell
types, and the two runs agree only loosely on which nuclei are most
contaminated. A group of nuclei, mostly in the epithelial cluster, come
out as almost entirely contamination with the empty droplets: their
counts look like the empty droplets'. They may be damaged nuclei, or
droplets holding mostly debris, and are worth a closer look before
further analysis.

## What each run removes

The two runs remove different genes. We compare the genes each run
removes, summed over all nuclei, with the genes in the empty droplets:

```{r removed_profiles}
removedShare <- function(decontaminated) {
  removed <- rowSums(counts(nuclei)) - rowSums(decontaminated)
  removed[removed < 0] <- 0
  removed / sum(removed)
}
profiles <- data.frame(
  emptyDroplets = rowSums(counts(empty)) / sum(counts(empty)),
  noBackground = removedShare(decontXcounts(noBackground)),
  withBackground = removedShare(decontXcounts(withBackground))
)
round(cor(sqrt(profiles)), 2)

topGenes <- function(share) {
  top <- head(sort(share, decreasing = TRUE), 10)
  paste0(names(top), " (", round(top * 100, 1), "%)")
}
data.frame(lapply(profiles, function(p) topGenes(setNames(p, rownames(nuclei)))))
```

With the empty droplets, what DecontX removes follows the empty droplets'
profile, which is led by mitochondrial transcripts, likely from the
cytoplasm stripped off during nuclei isolation. Without them, DecontX
removes MALAT1 most, and few mitochondrial transcripts: those are in
every cluster, so comparing clusters can't show that they are
contamination.

## Markers in the wrong cell types

We store both results in one object and plot the share of nuclei of each
cell type in which markers of other cell types are detected:

```{r marker_percentage, fig.width = 10, fig.height = 7}
assay(nuclei, "noBackground") <- decontXcounts(noBackground)
assay(nuclei, "withBackground") <- decontXcounts(withBackground)

markers <- list(
  Surfactant = c("SFTPC", "SFTPB"),
  Macrophage = c("MARCO", "CD163"),
  Fibroblast = c("COL1A2", "DCN"),
  Endothelial = c("PECAM1", "VWF"),
  Mitochondrial = c("MT-CO1", "MT-ND3")
)
groupClusters <- split(names(clusterType), clusterType)
plotDecontXMarkerPercentage(
  nuclei,
  markers = markers,
  groupClusters = groupClusters,
  assayName = c("counts", "noBackground", "withBackground"),
  z = z
)
```

Before decontamination, surfactant genes, which only the alveolar
epithelial cells make, are detected in three quarters or more of the
nuclei of every cell type. Both runs remove most of them from the other
cell types, but the run with the empty droplets removes more. It also
removes the mitochondrial transcripts from most nuclei, which the run
without them leaves in nearly all of them. The markers of fibroblasts,
endothelial cells and macrophages fall to a few percent outside their
own cell types in both runs.

# A comparison with CellBender

GEO also has the CellBender output for this sample, which estimates the
ambient RNA from the empty droplets as well. Neither method is ground
truth, but we can check which DecontX run comes closer to it:

```{r cellbender}
cbUrl <- paste0(
  geo, "GSM5230nnn/GSM5230057/suppl/",
  "GSM5230057_02-P005175-S053-R01_out_FPR_0.01_filtered.h5.gz"
)
cbH5 <- R.utils::gunzip(
  bfcrpath(bfc, cbUrl),
  destname = tempfile(fileext = ".h5"),
  remove = FALSE
)
cellbender <- read10xCounts(cbH5, col.names = TRUE, type = "HDF5")
stopifnot(identical(rowData(cellbender)$ID, rowData(nuclei)$ID))

shared <- intersect(colnames(nuclei), colnames(cellbender))
length(shared)
original <- counts(nuclei)[, shared]
cellbenderCounts <- as(counts(cellbender)[, shared], "dgCMatrix")
cellbenderRemoved <- 1 - colSums(cellbenderCounts) / colSums(original)

kept <- match(shared, colnames(nuclei))
round(c(
  noBackground = cor(estimates$noBackground[kept], cellbenderRemoved),
  withBackground = cor(estimates$withBackground[kept], cellbenderRemoved)
), 2)
```

These are the correlations, across nuclei, between each DecontX run's
contamination estimate and the share of counts CellBender removed. The
run with the empty droplets agrees with CellBender much better.

```{r cellbender_share}
round(c(
  CellBender = 1 - sum(cellbenderCounts) / sum(original),
  noBackground = 1 - sum(decontXcounts(noBackground)[, shared]) / sum(original),
  withBackground = 1 - sum(decontXcounts(withBackground)[, shared]) / sum(original)
), 3)
```

DecontX removes more counts in total than CellBender does at the false
positive rate used for these files (0.01).

# Summary

- Give DecontX the empty droplets when you have them and the
  contamination is high, as is common in single-nucleus data and in
  tissue that is hard to dissociate. Here they changed which nuclei
  DecontX called contaminated and which genes it removed.
- Leave out of the background the droplets you called as cells, including
  the ones that fail QC.
- With several samples, give each sample's empty droplets with `bgBatch`;
  see the vignette.

# Reference

Delorey TM, Ziegler CGK, Heimberg G, et al. COVID-19 tissue atlases reveal
SARS-CoV-2 pathology and cellular targets. *Nature* 595, 107–113 (2021).
<https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-03570-8>

